<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Socrates</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 470–<a href="/years/399-bce">399 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Socrates was an Athenian philosopher who wrote nothing himself but questioned his fellow citizens about justice, virtue and knowledge, a method of inquiry known through the dialogues of his pupil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>. In 399 BCE he was tried for impiety and corrupting the young, condemned and executed by drinking hemlock. He is regarded as a founder of Western philosophy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/socrates.jpg" alt="Socrates" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copy of Lysippos (?) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socrates_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Socrates</b>, tried and executed in 399 BCE, left his mark through his pupils; Plato founded the Academy around 387 BCE, Aristotle the Lyceum in 335 BCE, and Zeno and Epicurus opened their Stoic and Epicurean schools around 300 BCE, making Athens the philosophical capital of the Mediterranean into…</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Plato's Apology <b>Socrates</b> traces his philosophical mission to the Pythia's answer that no one was wiser than he.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Philosophers questioned the traditional gods: in the 6th century BCE Xenophanes mocked the idea of gods in human form, and in 399 BCE <b>Socrates</b> was condemned to death in Athens for impiety.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The full canon is described as taking in the Old and New Testaments, the Quran and works of Plato and of those influenced by <b>Socrates</b>, and Druze tradition counts Pythagoras, Plato and Aristotle among the five great prophets of the age of the ancient Greeks.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Aristotle himself warned that aristocracies slide into oligarchy when the few begin to rule for their own advantage, and in Plato’s Republic <b>Socrates</b> describes oligarchy as a city divided between the rich and the poor.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Plato, whose teacher <b>Socrates</b> was condemned to death by an Athenian jury of some 500 citizens in 399 BCE on charges of impiety and corrupting the young, compared the democratic city to a ship whose crew ignore the true navigator and seize the helm, and argued in the…</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-424" aria-label="Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Socrates"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-424">What was here in 424 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…daily to discourse about virtue, and of those other things about which you hear me examining myself and others, is the greatest good of man, and that the unexamined life is not worth living…&#8221;</p><footer>Apology &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; Plato's account of his defence &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1656/pg1656-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Well, although I do not suppose that either of us knows anything really beautiful and good, I am better off than he is,—for he knows nothing, and thinks that he knows; I neither know nor think that I know.&#8221;</p><footer>Apology &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; Plato's account of his defence &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1656/pg1656-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Crito, I owe a cock to Asclepius; will you remember to pay the debt?&#8221;</p><footer>Phaedo &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; his last words, in Plato's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1658/pg1658-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The hour of departure has arrived, and we go our ways—I to die, and you to live. Which is better God only knows.&#8221;</p><footer>Plato, Apology &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; his last words to the jury that condemned him, as Plato gives them &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1656/pg1656-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am that gadfly which God has attached to the state, and all day long and in all places am always fastening upon you, arousing and persuading and reproaching you.&#8221;</p><footer>Plato, Apology &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; his defence at his trial, as Plato gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1656/pg1656-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Wherefore, O judges, be of good cheer about death, and know of a certainty, that no evil can happen to a good man, either in life or after death.&#8221;</p><footer>Plato, Apology &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; to the jurors who had voted to acquit him, as Plato gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1656/pg1656-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
